So this area, if it is a vernal pool that's identified for conservation,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:10.490
is recommended

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:13.895
to remain completely undeveloped undisturbed.

00:19:13.895 --> 00:19:18.935
You should have no pesticides, nothing coming in. It's really important that the vegetation,

00:19:19.015 --> 00:19:23.335
the actual shape of the basin and the wallater quality remain undisturbed

00:19:23.335 --> 00:19:27.415
so that this can remain a high quality habitat for breeding to be successful.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:35.250
And then what you see in the center in that red is a 100 foot border from the edge of the depression of

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:38.370
the vernal pool, and this is known as the envelope.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:47.675
And this is essentially the critical habitat for breeding amphibians during the spring, and then it becomes critical habitat for their emerging juveniles during the fall.

00:19:48.315 --> 00:20:00.690
So this area is really important also as a water quality buffer. So if you have forest around the pool, that's going to prevent a lot of pollution from being able to enter the pool and then alter its ability to serve as successful breeding habitat.

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:02.210
So this

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:06.610
area as like the depression needs a lot of maintenance

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:09.810
in terms of remaining undisturbed

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:11.010
vegetation

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:17.335
on the understory as well as the canopy should be completely maintained. And And this is also really important because

00:20:17.415 --> 00:20:24.695
the leaf litter that's created by the canopy and the understory is really important for making sure that when you have those newly emerging juveniles,

00:20:24.855 --> 00:20:26.135
especially salamanders,

00:20:26.135 --> 00:20:28.215
they're not going to be desiccated

00:20:28.615 --> 00:20:29.735
from lack of shade.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:31.870
So this is a high

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:35.070
priority conservation zone as well.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:45.230
And then this last zone you're looking at in yellow is what's known as the critical terrestrial habitat. And this is a seven fifty foot border that's mapped from the edge of the Vernal Pool Depression.

00:20:46.555 --> 00:20:51.435
And essentially this is the upland habitat for species when it's not the breeding season.

00:20:52.075 --> 00:20:54.875
And it also shows the metapopulation

00:20:55.035 --> 00:21:05.840
aspects of vernal pools because you don't have species just using single vernal pools, you can have movement between them. And this is really important to ensuring the success because if one pool doesn't end up successful,

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.200
there's chances for

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:08.960
breeding in other pools.

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:10.880
So as

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:18.975
